WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.903
Task statement 4.3.

2
00:00:01.903 --> 00:00:06.187
Enforce structured output using tool use and JSON schemas.

3
00:00:06.187 --> 00:00:15.199
A downstream system crashes on one malformed JSON reply a day; tool use with a schema removes that whole class of failure.

4
00:00:16.599 --> 00:00:22.732
If you ask for JSON in plain words, you will occasionally get a missing bracket or an extra sentence.

5
00:00:22.732 --> 00:00:29.352
A more reliable route is to define a tool whose input schema is the shape you want, then have Claude call it.

6
00:00:29.352 --> 00:00:31.963
That is like a paper form with fixed boxes.

7
00:00:31.963 --> 00:00:36.396
It fixes the shape, but someone can still write the wrong thing in a box.

8
00:00:37.796 --> 00:00:42.894
The tool needs a name, a description, and an input schema written in JSON Schema.

9
00:00:42.894 --> 00:00:48.432
Required fields must appear, types are checked, and enums limit a field to known values.

10
00:00:48.432 --> 00:00:54.789
When Claude responds, you read the input of its tool use block, which already matches your structure.

11
00:00:54.789 --> 00:00:56.740
No fragile text parsing needed.

12
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.194
Tool choice controls whether a call is guaranteed.

13
00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:03.881
With auto, Claude might just answer in text.

14
00:01:03.881 --> 00:01:06.996
With any, it must call one of your tools, its pick.

15
00:01:06.996 --> 00:01:09.989
With a forced tool, it must call that exact tool.

16
00:01:09.989 --> 00:01:16.280
For an extraction step that must always produce structured output, any or forced is the guide's answer.

17
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:20.433
Current docs suggest auto with strict tool use on the newest models.

18
00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:23.622
Schema design shapes honesty.

19
00:01:23.622 --> 00:01:32.074
If a field is required and cannot be null, Claude is pushed to fill it even when the document says nothing, so make such fields nullable.

20
00:01:32.074 --> 00:01:36.331
Give enums an unclear option and an other option with a detail field.

21
00:01:36.331 --> 00:01:42.932
And state normalisation rules next to the schema, such as dates in one format and amounts as plain numbers.

22
00:01:44.332 --> 00:01:47.893
In the extraction scenario, a pipeline reads contracts.

23
00:01:47.893 --> 00:01:52.942
Many have no renewal date, so the field is nullable and null means not stated.

24
00:01:52.942 --> 00:01:57.668
Clause types use an enum with unclear and other, plus a free text detail.

25
00:01:57.668 --> 00:02:04.855
The extraction tool is forced, so every document produces a structured record that downstream systems can load.

26
00:02:06.255 --> 00:02:07.546
Three traps dominate.

27
00:02:07.546 --> 00:02:10.314
Believing that valid JSON means correct data.

28
00:02:10.314 --> 00:02:14.188
Making every field mandatory, which encourages invented values.

29
00:02:14.188 --> 00:02:17.693
And picking auto when the step must always call the tool.

30
00:02:17.693 --> 00:02:26.180
Remember the split: the schema guarantees shape, your validation checks meaning, and tool choice controls whether the call happens at all.

31
00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:32.723
Use a tool with a JSON schema whenever downstream code needs reliable structure.

32
00:02:32.723 --> 00:02:36.387
Choose tool choice to match how certain the call must be.

33
00:02:36.387 --> 00:02:41.144
Design the schema so Claude can be honest about missing or ambiguous data.

34
00:02:41.144 --> 00:02:46.801
Then check the values separately, because a perfect shape can still hold a wrong number.

